The pressure drop (ΔP) in a packed bed is directly proportional to the pressure gradient in the bed. When the pressure drop is divided by the bed void fraction (\(\epsilon\)), the gradient \(g(NRe)\) is calculated as:  

\[
g(NRe) = \frac{\Delta P}{\epsilon g}
\]

The pressure gradient is calculated as:  

\[
g = \frac{\Delta P}{L}
\]

The pressure drop is directly proportional to the bed length (\(L\)) and the pressure gradient (\(g\)). At low Reynolds numbers, inertial forces are negligible, and the pressure drop is proportional to the bed void fraction (\(\epsilon\)) to the power of 0.5, indicating that the dominant force is viscous. As the Reynolds number increases, inertial forces become dominant, and the pressure drop becomes proportional to the bed length (\(L\)) and the pressure gradient (\(g\)), with the effect of the bed void fraction (\(\epsilon\)) diminishing. This behavior is characteristic of the transition from viscous to inertial flow.  

Thus, the pressure drop in a packed bed is directly proportional to the bed length (\(L\)) and the pressure gradient (\(g\)), with the effect of the bed void fraction (\(\epsilon\)) diminishing as the Reynolds number increases.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{For low NRe (visicose regime):} \quad g(NRe) \cong C \frac{L}{\epsilon^{0.5}} \\
&\text{For high NRe (inersive regime):} \quad g(NRe) \cong C \frac{L}{L}
\end{aligned}
}

